<BaseLayout title="Research methodology — Payment Stack Guide" description="How Payment Stack Guide classifies providers, verifies claims, models fees, and handles uncertainty.">
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Trust before traffic" title="How the guide is researched" description="A provider page is only useful when its claims have dates, sources, scope, and honest uncertainty." meta="Review policy · version 1" />
  <section class="section"><div class="shell methodology-grid">
    <article><span>01</span><h2>Classify the legal seller first.</h2><p>A provider is labeled Merchant of Record only when first-party terms or documentation say it becomes the seller or reseller for the transaction. Tax calculation, billing software, fraud tools, and checkout products do not qualify on their own.</p></article>
    <article><span>02</span><h2>Prefer primary evidence.</h2><p>Pricing pages, contracts, eligibility documentation, API documentation, and official support policies support factual claims. Vendor comparisons can reveal questions, but they do not establish a competitor's facts.</p></article>
    <article><span>03</span><h2>Keep price formulas honest.</h2><p>Public, negotiated, method-based, country-specific, and contradictory pricing are separate states. The calculator exposes assumptions and refuses to produce exact estimates when the documentation does not support one.</p></article>
    <article><span>04</span><h2>Review changing facts.</h2><p>Every profile shows a last-reviewed and next-review date. Preview status, seller countries, product restrictions, fees, and migration behavior receive shorter review windows when they are changing quickly.</p></article>
    <article><span>05</span><h2>Do not rank without a model.</h2><p>There are no stars, universal winners, or unexplained scores. Recommendations name the business scenario, retained responsibilities, documented tradeoffs, and unknowns.</p></article>
    <article><span>06</span><h2>Separate information from advice.</h2><p>This publication explains provider documentation and operating choices. It is not legal, tax, or accounting advice. Verify contracts and obligations with qualified advisers for your business.</p></article>
    <article><span>07</span><h2>Label stories as stories.</h2><p>Founder accounts, Reddit discussions, engineering posts, and customer stories can reveal questions and possible failure modes. They do not establish frequency, current provider policy, or every side of a disputed event.</p></article>
    <article><span>08</span><h2>Keep the evidence hierarchy visible.</h2><p>Contracts and official documentation support current factual claims. Engineering accounts explain implementation. Customer and community stories add lived context. Promotional comparisons remain prompts for independent verification.</p></article>
    <article><span>09</span><h2>Do not confuse a product with its parent.</h2><p>Company records label whether financial figures describe the provider, a product segment, or a public parent. A parent's revenue and headcount show resources, not the economics or staffing of one checkout product.</p></article>
    <article><span>10</span><h2>Prefer an honest blank to a private-company estimate.</h2><p>Funding announcements, valuation events, payment volume, and customer counts are not revenue. When current revenue, profit, team size, or acquisition terms are not published, the guide says so explicitly.</p></article>
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
